Relevant columns and their potential usage

• batsman_scored  
  – match_id / over_id / innings_no : keys to aggregate per‑over runs.  
  – runs_scored : runs by the batsman to be summed.

• extra_runs  
  – match_id / over_id / innings_no : keys to aggregate per‑over extras.  
  – extra_runs : runs to be added to the batsman total.

• ball_by_ball  
  – match_id / over_id / innings_no : keys to identify the same over.  
  – bowler : bowler id for every delivery (normally the same for the whole over; will be used to fetch the bowler of the max‑run over).  

• player  
  – player_id : joins to ball_by_ball.bowler.  
  – player_name : readable bowler name.

• match  
  – match_id : outer frame to make sure we look at every match once and, at the end, compute the average of the single most expensive over per match.

Typical join / filter conditions

1. batsman_scored.match_id  = extra_runs.match_id  
2. batsman_scored.over_id   = extra_runs.over_id  
3. batsman_scored.innings_no= extra_runs.innings_no    (to add runs)  

4. (batsman_scored or extra_runs).match_id = ball_by_ball.match_id  
5. …over_id = ball_by_ball.over_id and …innings_no = ball_by_ball.innings_no  
6. ball_by_ball.bowler = player.player_id            (for bowler name)  

7. Inside each (match_id, innings_no) pick the MAX(total_runs) over.  
8. Aggregate those “max‑run overs” per match and finally AVG(total_runs).

Exploratory SQLs (10 queries, each DIFFERENT, all limited to 20 rows)

```sql
-- 1. Distinct innings numbers present in ball_by_ball
SELECT DISTINCT innings_no
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct over numbers recorded for a sample match (first match_id in table)
SELECT DISTINCT over_id
FROM ball_by_ball
WHERE match_id = (SELECT MIN(match_id) FROM ball_by_ball)
ORDER BY over_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Batsman runs per over for that same match (innings 1)
SELECT match_id, over_id, SUM(runs_scored) AS batsman_runs
FROM batsman_scored
WHERE match_id = (SELECT MIN(match_id) FROM batsman_scored)
  AND innings_no = 1
GROUP BY match_id, over_id
ORDER BY over_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Extra runs per over for the same match (innings 1)
SELECT match_id, over_id, SUM(extra_runs) AS extras
FROM extra_runs
WHERE match_id = (SELECT MIN(match_id) FROM extra_runs)
  AND innings_no = 1
GROUP BY match_id, over_id
ORDER BY over_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Combine batsman and extra runs to get total runs per over (innings 1, one sample match)
SELECT b.match_id,
       b.over_id,
       COALESCE(SUM(b.runs_scored),0) + COALESCE(SUM(e.extra_runs),0) AS total_runs
FROM batsman_scored AS b
LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
  ON b.match_id   = e.match_id
 AND b.over_id    = e.over_id
 AND b.innings_no = e.innings_no
WHERE b.match_id = (SELECT MIN(match_id) FROM batsman_scored)
  AND b.innings_no = 1
GROUP BY b.match_id, b.over_id
ORDER BY total_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Find the single highest‑scoring over for each match & innings
SELECT x.match_id,
       x.innings_no,
       MAX(x.total_runs) AS highest_over_runs
FROM (
     SELECT b.match_id,
            b.innings_no,
            b.over_id,
            COALESCE(SUM(b.runs_scored),0) + COALESCE(SUM(e.extra_runs),0) AS total_runs
     FROM batsman_scored AS b
     LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
       ON b.match_id   = e.match_id
      AND b.over_id    = e.over_id
      AND b.innings_no = e.innings_no
     GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id
) AS x
GROUP BY x.match_id, x.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Which bowler delivered those max‑run overs?  (showing just one match for clarity)
WITH per_over AS (
     SELECT b.match_id,
            b.innings_no,
            b.over_id,
            COALESCE(SUM(b.runs_scored),0) + COALESCE(SUM(e.extra_runs),0) AS total_runs
     FROM batsman_scored AS b
     LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
       ON b.match_id   = e.match_id
      AND b.over_id    = e.over_id
      AND b.innings_no = e.innings_no
     GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id
), max_over AS (
     SELECT po.*
     FROM per_over AS po
     JOIN (
          SELECT match_id, innings_no, MAX(total_runs) AS mx
          FROM per_over
          GROUP BY match_id, innings_no
     ) AS m
     ON po.match_id = m.match_id
    AND po.innings_no = m.innings_no
    AND po.total_runs = m.mx
)
SELECT mo.match_id,
       mo.innings_no,
       mo.over_id,
       mo.total_runs,
       bb.bowler
FROM max_over AS mo
JOIN ball_by_ball AS bb
  ON mo.match_id   = bb.match_id
 AND mo.over_id    = bb.over_id
 AND mo.innings_no = bb.innings_no
GROUP BY mo.match_id, mo.innings_no;  -- bowler is unique per over
```

```sql
-- 8. Bring in bowler names as well (still one match just for display)
WITH per_over AS (
     SELECT b.match_id,
            b.innings_no,
            b.over_id,
            COALESCE(SUM(b.runs_scored),0) + COALESCE(SUM(e.extra_runs),0) AS total_runs
     FROM batsman_scored AS b
     LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
       ON b.match_id   = e.match_id
      AND b.over_id    = e.over_id
      AND b.innings_no = e.innings_no
     GROUP BY b.match_id, b.innings_no, b.over_id
), max_over AS (
     SELECT po.*
     FROM per_over AS po
     JOIN (
          SELECT match_id, innings_no, MAX(total_runs) AS mx
          FROM per_over
          GROUP BY match_id, innings_no
     ) AS m
     ON po.match_id = m.match_id
    AND po.innings_no = m.innings_no
    AND po.total_runs = m.mx
), bowler_of_over AS (
     SELECT mo.match_id,
            mo.innings_no,
            mo.over_id,
            mo.total_runs,
            bb.bowler
     FROM max_over AS mo
     JOIN ball_by_ball AS bb
       ON mo.match_id   = bb.match_id
      AND mo.over_id    = bb.over_id
      AND mo.innings_no = bb.innings_no
     GROUP BY mo.match_id, mo.innings_no   -- bowler consistent within over
)
SELECT bo.match_id,
       bo.innings_no,
       bo.over_id,
       bo.total_runs,
       p.player_name AS bowler_name
FROM bowler_of_over AS bo
JOIN player AS p
  ON bo.bowler = p.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Highest‑scoring over per match (regardless of innings) and its bowler
WITH per_over AS (
     SELECT b.match_id,
            b.over_id,
            b.innings_no,
            COALESCE(SUM(b.runs_scored),0) + COALESCE(SUM(e.extra_runs),0) AS total_runs
     FROM batsman_scored AS b
     LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
       ON b.match_id   = e.match_id
      AND b.over_id    = e.over_id
      AND b.innings_no = e.innings_no
     GROUP BY b.match_id, b.over_id, b.innings_no
), max_over AS (
     SELECT po.*
     FROM per_over AS po
     JOIN (
          SELECT match_id, MAX(total_runs) AS mx
          FROM per_over
          GROUP BY match_id
     ) AS m
     ON po.match_id = m.match_id
    AND po.total_runs = m.mx
), bowler_of_over AS (
     SELECT mo.match_id,
            mo.over_id,
            mo.total_runs,
            bb.bowler
     FROM max_over AS mo
     JOIN ball_by_ball AS bb
       ON mo.match_id   = bb.match_id
      AND mo.over_id    = bb.over_id
      AND mo.innings_no = bb.innings_no
     GROUP BY mo.match_id                 -- ensure one row per match
)
SELECT bo.match_id,
       bo.over_id,
       bo.total_runs,
       p.player_name AS bowler_name
FROM bowler_of_over AS bo
JOIN player AS p
  ON bo.bowler = p.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Finally, compute the average runs of those single highest‑run overs across ALL matches
WITH per_over AS (
     SELECT b.match_id,
            b.over_id,
            b.innings_no,
            COALESCE(SUM(b.runs_scored),0) + COALESCE(SUM(e.extra_runs),0) AS total_runs
     FROM batsman_scored AS b
     LEFT JOIN extra_runs AS e
       ON b.match_id   = e.match_id
      AND b.over_id    = e.over_id
      AND b.innings_no = e.innings_no
     GROUP BY b.match_id, b.over_id, b.innings_no
), max_over AS (
     SELECT po.match_id,
            MAX(po.total_runs) AS highest_over_runs
     FROM per_over AS po
     GROUP BY po.match_id
)
SELECT AVG(highest_over_runs) AS avg_highest_over_runs_across_matches
FROM max_over;
```

The progressively complex queries above examine:

1. basic distributions (innings, overs)  
2. per‑over batsman and extra runs  
3. combining the run sources  
4. isolating the maximum‑run over per match/innings  
5. attaching the responsible bowler and his name  
6. finally producing the requested overall average of those peak overs.
